  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure building into the region from the west will \nremain over the Great Lakes through Wednesday, supporting dry \nweather for the next 24-30 hours. There may be a brief period \nthis evening with relatively clear or mostly clear conditions \nthat could allow for temperatures to drop in a slightly \ndecoupling atmosphere and lows could achieve the 20s for most. \nHowever, temperatures will moderate with increasing clouds and \nhigh pressure quickly moving east through the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe main concern for the entire forecast period will be a large,\noccluding low pressure system moving up the East Coast for\nThursday and Friday. The low pressure system will start as a\nstrong low over the southeast United States. As the system moves\nnorth and occludes over the region, the low will morph with one\npiece of the system aligning more closely with the closed upper\nlow over the central Appalachians and another piece moving \nnortheast with the shortwave ahead of the closed upper low\nthrough the New England states. This will allow for the local\narea to be between the two phases of the system, which will keep\nthe area wet Thursday and Friday, but not as cold, maintaining\nmostly rain. This will also keep the windiest portion of the\nsystem to the east. Overall, winds will still be strong across\nthe region with gusts of 30 to 40 mph and stronger over the lake\nwith the northeast flow. On Friday evening, rain could try to\nchangeover to some wet snow with 850 mb temperatures coming down\nto the -2 to -4 C range with the back edge of the low departing\nthe region. The most favorable areas will be the upslope\nlocations and higher elevations of Northeast Ohio and Northwest\nPennsylvania. Rain could continue into Saturday and Sunday with\nsome residual troughiness across the region.",
